.TH "UMAMI" "3" "2026" "umami 0.2.0" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
umami \- Rust library for structural log filtering
.SH SYNOPSIS
For a sibling source checkout:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][dependencies]\f[R]
umami = { package = \(dqumami\-log\(dq, path = \(dq../umami/umami\(dq }
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]use\f[R] umami::{AnomalyKind, Detector};
\f[B]let\f[R] \f[B]mut\f[R] detector = Detector::new();
assert_eq!(detector.observe(\(dqhost\(dq, \(dqstatus 200\(dq, 0).unwrap().kind,
           AnomalyKind::NeverBeforeSeen);
assert!(detector.observe(\(dqhost\(dq, \(dqstatus 204\(dq, 1).is_none());
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The umami\-log package exports the umami library.
The crates.io package named umami belongs to another project.
The library forbids unsafe code and has no required runtime
dependencies.
Enable the serde feature to serialize learned profiles.
.PP
The detector groups events by exact structure and reports novelty,
unusual minute counts, stable\-parameter changes, and silence.
It does not classify message meaning or guarantee that a recurring known
failure produces an alert.
.SH TOKENIZATION
\f[B]tokenize(line)\f[R] and
\f[B]Tokenizer::Structure.tokenize(line)\f[R] produce typed tokens.
The Legacy tokenizer has been removed.
The retained tokenizer recognizes timestamps, IPv4 addresses, numbers,
quoted contents, JSON keys, and key=value pairs.
.PP
\f[B]Token::is_parameter()\f[R] also classifies variable\-looking
literals, including digits, paths, email addresses, and long hexadecimal
identifiers.
Type\-level predicates do not inspect literal text.
\f[B]structure_signature()\f[R] hashes fixed text and parameter
placeholders in position order; \f[B]structure_string()\f[R] renders
that structure.
.PP
Signatures use 64\-bit FNV\-1a, not a cryptographic hash.
Version 0.2 changes some signatures; applications must not silently
reuse incompatible persistent profiles.
.SH DETECTOR
\f[B]Detector::new()\f[R] creates a default detector.
\f[B]with_config(config)\f[R] changes thresholds; construct the
non\-exhaustive configuration from \f[B]DetectorConfig::default()\f[R].
\f[B]with_store(config, store)\f[R] resumes learned profiles.
.PP
\f[B]observe(system, line, now)\f[R] tokenizes and observes one event.
\f[B]observe_tokens()\f[R] accepts previously tokenized input.
Both return \f[B]Option\f[R].
Pass Unix seconds; callers choose and validate their time source.
.PP
\f[B]sweep(now)\f[R] reports silent periodic events and absent systems.
Call it periodically; no library background thread advances time.
Per\-system lag adjustment prevents a consistently delayed system from
appearing absent just because other systems run ahead.
\f[B]clock()\f[R] returns the latest detector time, and
\f[B]store()\f[R] borrows its profiles.
.PP
Older events do not move last_seen backward or update timing statistics.
They still contribute counts and parameter learning.
Profiles remain allocated until the owning application discards the
store.
.SH LEARNING
Frequency detection compares a current minute\(cqs count with
completed\-minute statistics.
It requires thirty completed buckets and uses a variance floor of
max(variance, mean, 1).
The default \f[B]spike_threshold\f[R] is 3.
A spike reports a threshold crossing immediately; it does not grade the
eventual flood or estimate incident probability.
.PP
A periodic event requires at least twenty intervals with standard
deviation at most one fifth of their mean.
\f[B]stop_factor\f[R] defaults to 3.
Return gaps train the interval statistics, so a long outage can delay
renewed periodic eligibility.
Stop and spike alerts have separate rolling 86400\-second cooldowns per
system and structure.
A continuous silence remains one episode.
.PP
\f[B]away_secs\f[R] defaults to 600.
\f[B]learn_threshold\f[R] defaults to 1 and adds an observation count
requirement to frequency and parameter learning; it does not suppress
novelty.
.PP
\f[B]detect_parameter_changes\f[R] defaults to false.
Disabled detectors do not collect parameter values.
Enabled detectors exclude timestamp tokens and require twenty preceding
observations with distinct values at most one tenth of the observation
count.
More than 100 distinct values permanently saturates a slot and releases
its stored values.
.SH RESULTS
\f[B]Anomaly\f[R] contains system, kind, optional structure hash,
signature, original line, score, and \f[B]changed_params\f[R].
The latter contains zero\-based dynamic\-slot ordinals; excluded
timestamp slots still occupy an ordinal.
Stop and away events have no source line.
System\-away events also have no structure hash.
.PP
\f[B]AnomalyKind\f[R] is non\-exhaustive.
Its variants are \f[B]NeverBeforeSeen\f[R], \f[B]NewInSystem\f[R],
\f[B]FrequencySpike\f[R], \f[B]ParameterChange\f[R],
\f[B]EventStop\f[R], and \f[B]SystemAway\f[R].
\f[B]as_str()\f[R] returns stable lowercase names used by the CLI.
Scores are fixed values by kind, not measured probabilities.
There is no SpikeMagnitude grading in version 0.2.
.SH LOWER\-LEVEL TYPES
\f[B]ProfileStore\f[R] maps systems and hashes to profiles and records
global novelty.
\f[B]Profile\f[R] includes event counts, timestamps, parameter trackers,
interval statistics, and private persisted rate/cooldown state.
.PP
\f[B]RunningStats\f[R] maintains count, mean, and squared deviations.
\f[B]push()\f[R], \f[B]variance()\f[R], \f[B]stddev()\f[R], and
\f[B]zscore()\f[R] provide online summary calculations.
.PP
\f[B]ParamTracker::observe()\f[R] reports a new value only after stable
learning; unlike version 0.1, its boolean does not signal every
first\-seen value.
\f[B]parameter_values()\f[R] excludes timestamps while preserving
dynamic\-slot numbering.
.PP
\f[B]Pretty\f[R] supports Test, Colors, Params, Delimiters, and Static
rendering.
.PP
\f[B]time::parse_timestamp()\f[R] and \f[B]time::event_time()\f[R] parse
supported timestamp formats into Unix seconds.
Initial yearless inference uses a reference date.
Continuing input requires the explicit continuing\-year context;
arbitrary reordering without years remains ambiguous.
Zone\-less input uses UTC.
